fix(a11y): a muted role of this site's own, a button that stops moving, a way past the bar

Three defects, measured on the built site by the previous session and fixed
here rather than re-measured.

--overlay is the Face's muted role and clears the Face's bar, which is 3:1 —
a terminal's bar, and WCAG's bar for large text. This site then spent it on
standfirsts, captions, nav links, table keys and the theme label, none of
them within sight of 24px: 3.36:1 on base in mocha, 3.59:1 on the top bar's
mantle, and a color-contrast failure on every page. The role is not wrong;
the usage was. So tokens.css derives a seventeenth token instead of moving a
Face value — --muted, --text mixed toward --base, which cannot be a hex that
is right in one scheme and wrong in the other. The two percentages differ
because the two palettes do: mocha's text has 11.34:1 of headroom on its own
base and latte's has 7.06:1, so one number would have muted one scheme and
blinded the other. srgb mix is a per-channel lerp, so contrast.test.mjs
recomputes exactly what the browser paints and holds it to 4.5:1 against
both base and mantle — mantle because latte's is darker than its base, which
makes the top bar the tightest surface on the site at 4.56:1. tokens.test.mjs
fails the build on any `color: var(--overlay)` outside the social cards,
whose type scale has a 24px floor for this precise reason.

The theme button showed three different words in a box sized to its
contents, so the box changed width twice — 12px when the script painted the
real label on load, dragging the whole nav row, and 3px on every flip. Every
candidate label now stacks in one CSS grid cell with the extras hidden, so
the box is settled before the first of them is chosen. Not a JS fix: the
first shift happens before any script has run. theme-toggle.test.mjs reads
the script's label map and the markup's sizer list and fails when they
disagree, because adding a fourth state to one and not the other brings the
shift back for that label alone.

And no page had a skip link, so a keyboard reader walked four top-bar links
on every one of twenty-one pages.

scripts/a11y.mjs is the gate for the half of this that does not need a
browser: lang, one identified main, a skip link whose target exists on the
page, labelled landmarks, an accessible name on every control, alt on every
image, no positive tabindex. It found the double-main in its own author's
prose — which is how the last thing here got measured. Astro keeps
`<!-- -->` and strips `{/* */}`; Base.astro's five HTML comments were 2,632
bytes on every page, 14.6% of one, and are Astro comments now.
